I don't have any slides because I  decided to talk like 18 minutes ago so  my name is Chris Pfeiffer and I'm the  head of data science at cluster truck  and so I thought I'd use this as an  opportunity to explain what makes us  different from a real restaurant and or  a traditional restaurant and how we use  data to our advantage so in a  traditional restaurant they have data  that they call the P mix or the product  mix and this is the main thing driving  their menu decisions and what this is is  just how many of each item on their menu  that they've sold each week and at  clustered truck when we launched it was  important for us to have that same P mix  so that our chefs could look at that and  the way that they're used to but we also  are able to go much deeper since we're a  vertically integrated digital-only  kitchen so really quickly how many  people have heard of cluster truck or  tried cluster  okay so closer truck is basically a tech  enabled delivery only kitchen so the  main difference between us and someone  like doordash or grub hub is that we  cook all our own food and manage our own  menu and our engineered 100% for  delivery instead of basically attaching  digital ordering and delivery to  existing restaurants so what we look at  in addition to the traditional P mix is  for a certain item if someone ordered it  for the first time what percentage of  the time do they come back and order  plus a truck again we also look at for a  certain item if somebody ordered that on  their first order what percent of the  time when they do order again are they  ordering that same item versus something  else and so if we use this to optimize  not only the most popular menu items but  the menu items that are more likely to  result in a longer term more valuable  customer so another thing we're able to  do differently than a traditional  restaurant by leveraging data is that in  a traditional restaurant if someone was  if something was wrong with your meal  you would you'd say that to your server  they would maybe go back to the kitchen  have them modify it tweak it and then  bring it back out but that data wouldn't  be getting captured anywhere well those  types of errors are even more critical  for us because our customer is not  eating our food at the restaurant  they're eating at their home or their  workplace and so what we've done is  engineered systems that allow when we  get issues through a customer experience  team we document classify and store all  those and our culinary team has custom  data tools to look through all the  different types of issues that are  happening what items have the most  issues which kitchens have the most  issues and how is that varying across  items so for example if the Indianapolis  kitchen is always nailing the apple with  chicken salad and the Columbus Ohio  kitchen is always messing that up and  has a high error rate we can look at  what are those two kitchens doing  differently and we can use this data to  drive action and actually result in a  better result for the customer so that's  another way we use data differently than  a traditional restaurant the other thing  we can do is in a restaurant you may  have no traceability to a transaction so  if someone pays with cash or if they pay  with card and then a different card next  time you don't really know how that  customer is behaving over the long run  we use customer data because you always  have to have a digital account to order  cluster truck we can track lifetime  value of customers and figure out where  our most valuable customers are coming  from and where they're getting delivery  to and then therefore push for  attraction in venues that are similar to  where the most valuable customers are  coming from not just the most customers  are coming from another way you use data  to our advantage differently than a  traditional restaurant and the  traditional restaurant you walk in and  they say we're on a weight right now  it's going to be about 20 minutes and  they're doing this by essentially just  like looking at their wait list and kind  of looking at their watch and making a  guess and most the time they're erring  on the side of caution so that you'll be  pleasantly surprised that your weigh  time was less than what it was but you  really don't know how long it's going to  take but when you come to cluster Trucks  website you have no visual clue of how  busy we are because you can't see it a  line out the door you can't see the  server's looking busy or anything like  that so what we do is we use machine  learning to predict to you how long it  will be if you were to place your order  right now for your food to get delivered  and we're able to do this much better  than our competitors like doordash and  GrubHub  because we're vertically integrated we  have insight into what everyone's  cooking how busy each station is how  many orders are being withheld until we  have the driver bandwidth and Driver  bandwidth or cook bandwidth to cook them  and so basically we're able to leverage  machine learning to predict our wait  times with an average error of about  four and a half minutes so that's some  of the ways that cluster truck leverages  data to be different than traditional  restaurants and we're still just kind of  at the beginning of understanding how  powerful what we can do with the data  what we have is thanks  [Music]
 

